== README R9's mechanism, from the JIT's own output rather than from inference ==

java.version=17.0.20  vm=17.0.20+8-1-24.04-Ubuntu  protobuf-java 3.25.5
payload P1.2, content set above U+00FF, encode only, `pbj` base ns/op.

`logs/java/deopt.log` established that the hazard is real, that it is on JDK 17
and not 21, that a LATIN1 probe triggers it and a WIDE one does not, and that the
C ABI arm is immune. What it could not establish was WHY, and it said so: the
mechanism was inferred from which arms move. This replaces the inference.

-XX:+TraceDeoptimization and -Xlog:deoptimization are not available on a product
build. -XX:+UnlockDiagnosticVMOptions -XX:+LogCompilation is, and it PRESERVES the
effect. -XX:StartFlightRecording=settings=profile ERASES it (642 us at deopt=0
against 1,261 without). So the first thing checked was whether the hazard survives
being watched, and which instrument it survives.

== 1. The mechanism, and it is not deoptimisation ==

Six logged runs. The C2 compilation of com.google.protobuf.Utf8$UnsafeProcessor
.encodeUtf8 has two String.charAt sites. Both inline String.isLatin1() and
StringLatin1.charAt in every run. What differs is the other branch:

  run                        pbj ns/op    StringUTF16.charAt at the two sites
  deopt=0 rep1               1,263,883    inline_fail 'call site not reached' x2
  deopt=0 rep2               1,279,672    inline_fail 'call site not reached' x2
  deopt=1 (String.format)    1,298,338    inline_fail 'call site not reached' x2
  deopt=2 (WIDE probe)       1,238,711    inline_fail 'call site not reached' x2
  deopt=3 (LATIN1 probe)       619,520    compiled in, intrinsic _getCharStringU x2
  deopt=3 rep2                 614,618    compiled in, intrinsic _getCharStringU x2

The payload's strings are all above U+00FF, so StringUTF16.charAt is the path the
measurement needs. In the slow state C2 compiled it out as unreachable.

Runtime uncommon_trap events, whole process: 9 at deopt=0 (4 unstable_if, 4
class_check, 1 loop_limit_check) and 11 at deopt=3. **Deoptimisation is not the
mechanism and the slow state has FEWER traps, not more.** R9's word for this is
wrong in this slice: it is a compile-time pruning decision.

== 2. The inferred mechanism in STATE.md is refuted ==

STATE.md said the two encoders 'are both charAt loops over a String and share one
compact-string dispatch profile, which can only be specialised one way'. They do
not. In the same six logs, ak.Utf8.encode and ak.Utf8.length compile IDENTICALLY
in all four modes: zero 'call site not reached', the UTF16 branch present, and
_getCharStringU applied, every time. Only protobuf-java's encoder is pruned.
Whatever moves arm R in deopt.log's table, it is not a shared profile.

== 3. The effect is bimodal and probabilistic, not a fixed penalty ==

Ten runs per mode, -Dak.only=P1.2, microseconds:

  mode        n     min  median     max   landed fast
  deopt=0     10     595    1257    1276    2 of 10
  deopt=1     10    1246    1273    1283    0 of 10
  deopt=2     10     603    1224    1268    1 of 10
  deopt=3     10     617     634     648   10 of 10

  deopt=0: 595 621 1245 1251 1252 1263 1273 1274 1275 1276
  deopt=1: 1246 1268 1270 1270 1272 1274 1280 1280 1283 1283
  deopt=2: 603 1196 1201 1218 1221 1227 1228 1229 1234 1268
  deopt=3: 617 620 625 627 630 638 638 638 644 648

There is no continuum. Every run lands at about 620 us or at about 1,250 us and
the gap between is empty. **The probe does not change the number, it changes the
probability of landing in the state where the branch survived.** The LATIN1 probe
makes it certain; without any probe the process gets there on its own about one
run in ten. `logs/java/deopt.log` recorded exactly one such stray reading under
mode 2 and declined to drop it; this is what that reading was.

A published table with one reading per cell was therefore reporting a MODE, not a
value, and could not have known it.

== 4. deopt=1, the mode R9 actually names, no longer reproduces ==

`logs/java/deopt.log` has four consecutive fast readings for mode 1 (586, 587,
606, 602 us). Here it is slow in 13 of 13 runs. The payload restriction does not
explain it: with the full payload set, P1.2 ns/op in microseconds

  deopt=0: 626 905 922
  deopt=1: 954 928 923
  deopt=3: 630 606 621

Mode 1 is slow there too. Mode 3 is fast in every configuration and every run.
Note also that the full payload set moves the SLOW value from about 1,250 us to
about 920: a more varied string diet before P1.2 partly saves the branch, which is
what the pruning account predicts.

What changed between deopt.log and here: the W10 re-gate onto the shared core and
the D7 takeBytes fix, both of which change code in the measured process. Attributing
the shift to one of them needs a bisection over a PROBABILISTIC outcome, so it is
recorded as unexplained rather than guessed at. If mode 1 had mode 0's roughly one
in ten chance, 0 fast in 13 runs has probability about 0.25; if it had deopt.log's
apparent certainty, it has probability zero. The shift is real.

== 5. What this means for the published correction ==

README section R9 carries this slice's correction already. Three of its four
clauses stand: JDK 17 and not 21, a LATIN1 read and not a numeric conversion, no
narrowing loop, nothing on ASCII. Two need changing and the slice cannot edit that
file: the mechanism is branch pruning rather than deoptimisation, and the effect is
a probability rather than a penalty. The request is filed in STATE.md.
